window.__NOTE_CONTENT = "<h2 id=\"researchmethods\">RESEARCHMETHODS</h2>\n<p>Nature and Meaning of Research</p>\n<p>Research is derived from two words Re and Search. Re means finding out again</p>\n<p>while Search means finding out as a result research simply mean finding out. The</p>\n<p>big question is what are we finding out? In research therefore we are interested in</p>\n<p>finding solution to problems.</p>\n<p>Research is a systematic process used by scholars to provide solution to problems;</p>\n<p>to uncover facts in an attempt to formulate rules and generalization based on the</p>\n<p>facts uncovered through approved investigative procedures.</p>\n<p>Research can be defined as a systematic way of finding out about the worth or</p>\n<p>otherwise of a given subject matter. Research has varied definitions depending on</p>\n<p>&#x27;viewpoints and usage’. It is finding out the truth about an event or situation. The</p>\n<p>Webster dictionary defines research as a careful or diligent search, studious and</p>\n<p>critical inquiry and examination aimed at the discovery and interpretation of new</p>\n<p>knowledge.</p>\n<p>According to Adewumi (1988) in Awoniyi, Aderanti and Tayo (2011),</p>\n<p>research is a process of finding out the aspect of knowledge or idea which people</p>\n<p>have not known about or they are not clear about them. It is also a process of</p>\n<p>finding out new information, new methods of doing things and an extension of</p>\n<p>boundary of knowledge. Osuala (1977) in Adewumi (1988) says research is simply</p>\n<p>the process of arriving at dependable solutions to problem through the planned</p>\n<p>and systematic collection and interpretation of data. Nwana (1985) describes</p>\n<p>research as a broad term which is a process of finding out the solution to a</p>\n<p>problem. The action word in this rather simple definition is the finding out or put</p>\n<p>in other words the ‘searching for something.</p>\n<p>It is clear from these selected descriptions that the existence of a problem or</p>\n<p>problems is a central core of what all researches is all about. No research can exist</p>\n<p>in the absence of a problem. Identification of problems is therefore the beginning</p>\n<p>of such activities either singly or by groups of people.</p>\n<p>In summary, research can be defined as an investigative process of finding reliable</p>\n<p>solution to a problem through a planned and systematic selection, collection,</p>\n<p>analysis and interpretation</p>\n<p>of data relating to the problem.</p>\n<p>What Research is not</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-research-is-not-simply-rewriting-other-people-s-words-or-ideas-into-a-neat\">1. Research is not simply rewriting other people’s words or ideas into a neat</h3>\n<p>description i.e. summarizing what is found in textbooks. It could only</p>\n<p>become a research when ideas are analyzed and organized into</p>\n<p>appropriate thought</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-research-is-not-a-defense-of-one-s-convictions-research-seeks-truth-and\">2. Research is not a defense of one’s convictions. Research seeks truth and</h3>\n<p>as a result the position must be based on truth.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-research-objective-is-to-clearly-present-the-truth-and-not-to-fight-others\">3. Research objective is to clearly present the truth and not to fight others</h3>\n<p>position</p>\n<p>Needs for Research</p>\n<p>There are many reasons for carrying out research, but perhaps the most important</p>\n<p>is to broaden and deepen what you know by allowing you to become a mini-expert</p>\n<h4 id=\"in-your-own-area-some-of-the-needs-for-research-are\">in your own area. Some of the needs for research are:</h4>\n<p>-   Provide training in problem solving</p>\n<p>-   It allows one to become expert in his area</p>\n<p>-   Helps in testing what you think you know. Just because someone told you</p>\n<p>something how do you know it is true? Research can help you find out</p>\n<p>-   Helps in understanding the other side of an issue. Research can force you to</p>\n<p>look at an issue from more than one perspective</p>\n<p>-   There are quite a number of research oriented jobs for which knowledge of</p>\n<p>research can make one to be gainfully employed</p>\n<p>-   Desire to get a degree along with its consequential benefits</p>\n<p>-</p>\n<h2 id=\"research-paradigm-approach-and-methods\">RESEARCH PARADIGM, APPROACH AND METHODS</h2>\n<p>Paradigm</p>\n<p>The term paradigm originated from the Greek word paradigm which means</p>\n<p>pattern and was first used by Thomas Kuhn (1962) to denote a conceptual</p>\n<p>framework shared by a community of scientists which provided them with a</p>\n<p>convenient model for examining problems and finding solutions. Kuhn defines a</p>\n<p>paradigm as: “an integrated cluster of substantive concepts, variables and problems</p>\n<p>attached with corresponding methodological approaches and tools…” According</p>\n<p>to him, the term paradigm refers to a research culture with a set of beliefs, values,</p>\n<p>and assumptions that a community of researchers has in common regarding the</p>\n<p>nature and conduct of research (Kuhn, 1977). A paradigm hence implies a pattern,</p>\n<p>structure and framework or system of scientific and academic ideas, values and</p>\n<p>assumptions.</p>\n<p>During the past century, different paradigms have taken birth due to the</p>\n<p>remarkable growth in social sciences research.</p>\n<h4 id=\"there-are-mainly-two-paradigms\">There are mainly two paradigms:</h4>\n<p>Positivism and</p>\n<p>Anti-positivism (or naturalistic inquiry)</p>\n<p>Positivism</p>\n<p>Positivist believes that true knowledge is based on experience of senses and can be</p>\n<p>obtained by observation and experiment. Positivistic thinkers adopt the scientific</p>\n<p>method as a means of knowledge generation</p>\n<p>Positivistic paradigm thus systematizes the knowledge generation process with the</p>\n<p>help of quantification, which is essential to enhance precision in the description of</p>\n<p>parameters and the discernment of the relationship among them. Positivist</p>\n<p>paradigm adopts the quantitative approach.</p>\n<p>Anti-positivism</p>\n<p>Anti-positivism emphasizes that social reality is viewed and interpreted by the</p>\n<p>individual according to the ideological positions possessed. The anti-positivists</p>\n<p>believe that reality is multi-layered and complex and a single phenomenon is having</p>\n<p>multiple interpretations.</p>\n<p>Anti-positivism involves probing into the various unexplored dimensions of a</p>\n<p>phenomenon rather than establishing specific relationship among the components.</p>\n<p>It emphasizes understanding and interpretation of phenomena and making</p>\n<p>meaning out of this process. It is basically qualitative in approach.</p>\n<p>Research Approach</p>\n<p>The research method is a strategy of enquiry, which moves from the underlying</p>\n<p>assumptions to research design, and data collection (Myers, 2009). Although there</p>\n<p>are other distinctions in the research modes, the most common classification of</p>\n<p>research methods is into qualitative</p>\n<p>and quantitative. The terms refer to research methods, that is, the way in which</p>\n<p>data are collected and analyzed, and the type of generalizations and representations</p>\n<p>derived from the data.</p>\n<p>Research Design</p>\n<p>Research Design can be thought of as the logic or master plan of a research that</p>\n<p>throws light on how the study is to be conducted. It shows how all of the major</p>\n<p>parts of the research study– the samples or groups, measures, treatments or</p>\n<p>programs, etc– work together in an attempt to address the research questions.</p>\n<p>Research design is similar to an architectural outline.</p>\n<p>The research design refers to the overall strategy that you choose to integrate the</p>\n<p>different components of the study in a coherent and logical way, thereby, ensuring</p>\n<p>you will effectively address the research problem; it constitutes the blueprint for</p>\n<p>the collection, measurement, and analysis of data</p>\n<p>A research design is the set of methods and procedures used in collecting and</p>\n<p>analyzing measures of the variables specified in the problem research.</p>\n<p>Table 1: Selection of research paradigms and research</p>\n<p>methods</p>\n<p>Research      Research       Research methods                  Examples</p>\n<p>paradigms     approach</p>\n<p>Positivism   Quantitative Surveys             - Attitude of distance</p>\n<p>learners towards online</p>\n<p>based education</p>\n<p>Correlational        - Relationship between</p>\n<p>students’ motivation and</p>\n<p>their academic achievement.</p>\n<p>Experimental         - Effect of intelligence on</p>\n<p>the academic</p>\n<p>performances of primary</p>\n<p>school learners</p>\n<p>Quasi-experimental</p>\n<p>The effect of Grade Zero</p>\n<p>and ex-post facto    attendance on achievement at</p>\n<p>the end of the first grade</p>\n<p>research</p>\n<p>Anti-            Qualitative      Biographical          - A study of autobiography</p>\n<p>positivism                                              of a great statesman.</p>\n<p>- A study of dropout among</p>\n<p>Phenomenological      the female students</p>\n<p>Ethnographical        - A study of the culture and</p>\n<p>educational practices among</p>\n<p>the Yorubas of Nigeria</p>\n<p>Case study            - A case study of an open</p>\n<p>distance learning Institution</p>\n<p>in a country.</p>\n<p>Critical         Critical and Ideology critique         - A study of development of</p>\n<p>theory           action-                                education during the British</p>\n<p>oriented                               rule in India</p>\n<p>Action research       - Absenteeism among</p>\n<p>standard five students of a</p>\n<p>primary school</p>\n<h2 id=\"classification-of-research\">CLASSIFICATION OF RESEARCH</h2>\n<h4 id=\"research-could-be-classified-based-on-the-following-criteria\">Research could be classified based on the following criteria:</h4>\n<p>i.        Classification by purpose</p>\n<p>ii.       Classification by method.</p>\n<p>iii.      Classification based on type of evidences required and method of analysis.</p>\n<p>Classification of Research by Purpose</p>\n<p>Classification of research by purpose is based primarily on the degree to which</p>\n<p>findings have direct application in education or the degree to which they are</p>\n<p>generalizable to other educational situations. Types of research based on purpose</p>\n<h4 id=\"or-use-are\">or use are:</h4>\n<p>-      Basic (Pure) research</p>\n<p>-   Applied research.</p>\n<p>-   Action research</p>\n<p>-   Evaluation research</p>\n<p>-   Research and Development (R &amp; D)</p>\n<p>Basic [Pure] Research: This is concerned with the production of result and</p>\n<p>finding which lead to development of theory. This type of research is not for</p>\n<p>finding solution to problem but rather interested in building theories which can</p>\n<p>lead to advancement of knowledge in a particular field. For example, much basic</p>\n<p>research has been conducted with animals to determine principles of</p>\n<p>reinforcement.</p>\n<p>Applied Research: As the name implies, itis conducted for the purpose of</p>\n<p>applying or testing theory. It is concerned with the usefulness of ideas or theories</p>\n<p>to practical situation. Applied research provides data to support theory, guide</p>\n<p>theory revision or suggest development of new theory. For example which forms</p>\n<p>of motivation brings about better academic performance among students?</p>\n<p>Action Research: This type of research is directed towards solving specific</p>\n<p>problem in a local setting. It is usually taken by a teacher or group of teachers in</p>\n<p>school to solve specific class room problem so that the result can be generalized.</p>\n<p>The primary goal of action research is to provide solution to a given problem not</p>\n<p>to contribute to knowledge. It is meant to provide immediate answers to problems</p>\n<p>that cannot wait for critical solutions. For example, repeating a lesson after</p>\n<p>realizing that students do not have a clear understanding of the topic.</p>\n<p>Evaluation Research: The results of evaluation research help in decision making.</p>\n<p>It helps to decide whether a programme is to be continued, modified or be</p>\n<p>stopped. Evaluation is traditionally classified according to its purpose namely</p>\n<p>formative and summative evaluation.</p>\n<p>Formative evaluation is used for the purpose of program improvement. It is carried</p>\n<p>out when a programme is in progress.</p>\n<p>Summative evaluation is used for the purpose of making summary judgments about a</p>\n<p>program and decisions to continue or discontinue the program.</p>\n<p>A newer and current popular way to classify evaluation research is to divide it into</p>\n<p>five types Needs assessment, which asks this question, is there a need for this type</p>\n<p>of program?</p>\n<p>Theory assessment, which asks this question, is this program conceptualized in a</p>\n<p>way that it should work?</p>\n<p>Implementation assessment, which asks: Was this program implemented properly</p>\n<p>and according to the program plan?</p>\n<p>Impact assessment, which asks: Did this program have an impact on its intended</p>\n<p>targets? Efficiency assessment, which asks: Is this program cost effective?</p>\n<p>Research and Development: This is aimed at developing and testing product to</p>\n<p>ensure their effectiveness. For example, the development of teaching and learning</p>\n<p>aids to improve assimilation of content by pupils/students.</p>\n<p>Classification of Research by Method</p>\n<p>This classification is based on the method or strategy to be used in the conduct of</p>\n<p>a research. All methods have certain procedure in common such as statement of a</p>\n<p>problem, collection of data, analysis of data and drawing conclusions. Classification</p>\n<p>by method appears to be the most efficient as it minimizes categories and</p>\n<p>maximizes differentiation. It places all research into five categories namely.</p>\n<p>-   Historical</p>\n<p>-   Descriptive (survey)</p>\n<p>-   Correlational</p>\n<p>-   Experimental and</p>\n<p>-   Casual-comparative (ex-post-facto)</p>\n<p>Historical Research</p>\n<p>This type of research is based on oral evidence, records such as diaries, case</p>\n<p>history, autobiography, logbook; Books, Journals, magazines etc. The purpose of</p>\n<p>an historical research is to obtain a better understanding of the present through the</p>\n<p>evaluation of the past and intelligent prediction of the future. An historical</p>\n<p>research aids in avoiding past errors and predicting the future. It also refreshes</p>\n<p>ones memory on what is known and unfold what is not known.</p>\n<p>There are two main sources of collecting historical data. They are primary and secondary</p>\n<p>sources. Primary sources are relics and other things that have direct physical relationship</p>\n<p>e.g. observation, participation etc. A secondary source deals with bibliographies,</p>\n<p>references,materials and documents recorded by someone else. These are less reliable</p>\n<p>than</p>\n<p>primary sources. In this type of research approach statistical hypotheses are rarely</p>\n<p>used. Example: Historical Development of Bugema University.</p>\n<p>Descriptive Research</p>\n<p>It is also called a survey research. This type of research is based on information,</p>\n<p>interviews, (oral, written, structured, unstructured etc), inventories, rating scales,</p>\n<p>self-report, observation etc. Descriptive research is aimed at fact finding of the</p>\n<p>present condition or current situation. The result obtained through this procedure</p>\n<p>can be statistically analyzed.A descriptive survey generally tries to collect</p>\n<p>information from a representative group and based on such information inferences</p>\n<p>are drawn about the behavior of the entire population.The descriptive survey is</p>\n<p>usually prompted by the need to know what the current situation is with regard to</p>\n<p>a particular educational planning problem, but it may not tell the researcher what</p>\n<p>has caused the problem nor will it supply alternative solutions.</p>\n<p>The major limitations of this research approach is found in the questions asked as</p>\n<p>people may not actually behave the way they respond to questions. Moreover, the</p>\n<p>descriptive survey also provides noindication of the relationship between the</p>\n<p>variables studied. They only provide information without explanation.</p>\n<p>Example of Descriptive survey .topics</p>\n<p>(a)   A survey of decision-making practices of secondary school principals.</p>\n<p>(b)   A study of university students’attitude to online registration.</p>\n<p>(c)   A study of factors affecting the teaching of Mathematics.</p>\n<p>Correlational Research</p>\n<p>The correlational studies present a research approach that seeksto explain</p>\n<p>educational phenomena rather than mere stating the phenomena. It also requires a</p>\n<p>representative sample, but in this case it demands much more sophisticated</p>\n<p>statistical analysis to be carried out. A correlational study provides evidence of</p>\n<p>relationship. The major advantage of correlational studies is that relationships are</p>\n<p>measured in their real setting. A major disadvantage of this</p>\n<p>approach is that since real life is complicated attempt to control certain variables</p>\n<p>and examine one or two aspects of behavior in isolation may be difficult.</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-following-are-examples-of-typical-correlational-studies\">The following are examples of typical correlational studies:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-relationship-between-intelligence-and-academic-performance-scores-on-an-intelligence\">1.     The relationship between intelligence and academic performance. Scores on an Intelligence</h3>\n<p>test and performance test would be acquired from each member of a given group. The two</p>\n<p>sets of scores would be correlated and the resulting coefficient would indicate the degree of</p>\n<p>relationship.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-use-of-entry-grades-to-predict-success-at-university-entry-grade-point-would-be-correlated\">2. . Use of entry grades to predict success at university. Entry grade point would be correlated</h3>\n<p>with ultimate Cumulative Grade Point Average (CGPA) at the end of university</p>\n<p>education. If the resulting coefficient is high, the entry grade would be considered a good</p>\n<p>predicator.</p>\n<p>Experimental Research</p>\n<p>This type of research involves conducting experiment for research purpose. The</p>\n<p>experimental method involves investigating possible cause and effect relationship</p>\n<p>by exposing one or more experimental groups to a treatment and one or more</p>\n<p>control groups not receiving the treatment</p>\n<p>The simplest form of experiment involves only two measures the dependent</p>\n<p>variables otherwise regarded as “effects” and the independent variables, otherwise</p>\n<p>known as causes. In an experiment two or more groups are necessary. These</p>\n<p>include a control group and at least one experimental group.</p>\n<p>To enable one to compare, the group must be as near identical as possible. The</p>\n<p>main problem faced in carrying out experimental research in education is that in</p>\n<p>trying to control the independent variable and setting up marched groups, one may</p>\n<p>be interfering with normal school process. This is why there are limitations to</p>\n<p>adoption of experimental research in education, since we cannot experiment on</p>\n<p>people in a way which may affect their lives adversely.</p>\n<h4 id=\"example-of-typical-experimental-studies\">Example of typical experimental studies:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-comparative-effectiveness-of-discussion-and-lecture-method-on-pupils\">1.   The comparative effectiveness of discussion and lecture method on pupils’</h3>\n<p>academic</p>\n<p>performance. The independent variable or “cause” is type of instruction</p>\n<p>(discussion versus lecture); the dependent variable or “effect” is academic</p>\n<p>performance. Two groups (preferably randomly formed) would be exposed</p>\n<p>to essential the same experienced except for method of institution. After</p>\n<p>some period of time, their academic performance would be compared.</p>\n<p>Causal-Comparative Research</p>\n<p>Causal-comparative and experimental research represent distinctly different</p>\n<p>methods, however both attempt to establish cause-effect relationship, and both</p>\n<p>involve group comparison. In causal-comparative study the independent variable</p>\n<p>or ‘cause’ is not manipulated, it has already occurred. Independent variables in</p>\n<p>causal-comparative studies are variables which cannot be manipulated e.g. gender,</p>\n<p>(male -female), location (urban-rural).</p>\n<p>The following are examples of typical causal-comparative studies</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-effect-of-grade-zero-attendance-on-achievement-at-the-end-of-the-first-grade\">1. The effect of Grade Zero attendance on achievement at the end of the first grade.</h3>\n<p>Theindependent variable or cause is Grade Zero attendance (students attended</p>\n<p>Grade Zero or they did not); the dependent variable or effect is the</p>\n<p>achievement at the end of the first grade. Two groups of first graders would be</p>\n<p>identified - one group which had attended Grade Zero, and one which had</p>\n<p>not. The achievement of the two groups would be compared.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-the-effect-of-gender-on-academic-achievement-of-secondary-school-students-the\">2. The effect of gender on academic achievement of secondary school students. The</h3>\n<p>independent variable or cause is gender (male and female); the dependent</p>\n<p>variable or effect is academic achievement. The achievement of males would be</p>\n<p>compared to the achievement of females.</p>\n<p>Developmental Research</p>\n<p>Developmental researches want actually to study the growth characteristics of a</p>\n<p>child. These growth characteristics include intellectual, emotional and physical. It</p>\n<p>aims at measuring growth of all these over time.</p>\n<p>Developmental research in Education involves a number of specific tasks of study</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-longitudinal-studies\">1.      Longitudinal studies</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-cross-sectional-studies\">2.      Cross-sectional studies</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-trend-predictive-studies\">3.    Trend/predictive studies</h3>\n<p>All of these measure development over time.</p>\n<p>Longitudinal Studies</p>\n<p>This term is used to describe a type of study conducted over a period of time</p>\n<p>which could be long or short. Data concerning the subject to be studied are</p>\n<p>gathered over a period of time. A short longitudinal study may take a week, while a</p>\n<p>long one might take years even up to fifty years. In longitudinal studies, the sample</p>\n<p>is constant throughout the study. This distinguishes longitudinal studies from other</p>\n<p>types of developmental research. Depending on the time, data are collected at</p>\n<p>intervals on the intended characteristics.</p>\n<p>There are two basic types of longitudinal studies;</p>\n<p>i.     Prospective longitudinal studies. Here the emphasis is beginning from a</p>\n<p>point and follow the sample into the future. The measurement is done into an</p>\n<p>established date in future.</p>\n<p>ii.    Retrospective longitudinal studies. Here the emphasis is to establish a</p>\n<p>point and then go into the past of the particular sample. See what happen to the</p>\n<p>sample in the past. This type is common in the area of psychology and Guidance</p>\n<p>and Counseling. Looking into the past to know what contributed to their present</p>\n<p>state. Retrospective longitudinal studies are less reliable as one need to collect data</p>\n<p>from those associated with the sample.</p>\n<p>Advantages of Longitudinal Studies</p>\n<p>i. The longitudinal studies unlike other type of developmental research is able to</p>\n<p>identify typical patterns of development and find out factors that determine</p>\n<p>development in individual. It enables one to establish general relation. It enables</p>\n<p>the researcher to examine individual variation in characteristics or traits and</p>\n<p>produce growth curves for development of individual and rate of growth.</p>\n<p>ii. Time which is usually a limiting factor in research is generally more readily</p>\n<p>available in longitudinal studies.</p>\n<p>Disadvantages</p>\n<p>i      They are time consuming and resource consuming and as a result only rich</p>\n<p>researchers can carry out longitudinal studies, as samples has to be transferred</p>\n<p>wherever there is cause for movement.</p>\n<p>ii.   Sample mortality: There is probability of death of some of the subjects no</p>\n<p>matter how short the time might be and depending on the size of the sample.</p>\n<p>Once sample die one cannot choose another sample. The choice of a new sample</p>\n<p>will affect the result of the research.</p>\n<p>iii   They impose considerable problems of organization on the researcher e.g.</p>\n<p>Keeping track on the sample or keeping contact with the sample. Longitudinal</p>\n<p>studies are the most important of all development researches.</p>\n<p>Cross - Sectional Studies</p>\n<p>Cross-sectional study is one in which different respondents are studied at different</p>\n<p>point in time. A cross-sectional study is often referred as a &quot;snapshot&quot; of a</p>\n<p>population at a particular point in time. Here the samples are not constant and we</p>\n<p>are not concerned about an individual. On the basis of cross-sectional one will be</p>\n<p>able to determine development at different ages or time.</p>\n<p>A major advantage of Cross-sectional studies over longitudinal studies is that</p>\n<p>of time. While longitudinal studies will study over several years, cross-sectional</p>\n<p>study might be concluded within a year. Longitudinal studies are more reliable as</p>\n<p>the same sample is been used and might not be affected by circumstance as when</p>\n<p>different samples are used.</p>\n<p>Trend/Predictive Studies</p>\n<p>The trend/predictive studies attempt to examine recorded data to establish pattern</p>\n<p>of changes. With the past trend this type of study can predict into the future. The</p>\n<p>trend/predictive studies is not limited to individual or sample alone but can be</p>\n<p>used even for the development of a population.</p>\n<p>Advantages</p>\n<p>I      More economical in term of time.</p>\n<p>ii.    Bring quick results.</p>\n<p>Disadvantages</p>\n<p>i.     Reasonable sample needed.</p>\n<p>ii.    Not easy to draw conclusion as circumstances might change</p>\n<h2 id=\"variables-in-research\">VARIABLES IN RESEARCH</h2>\n<p>The critical factors of an investigation are known as variable. A variable is anything</p>\n<p>that is changeable or alterable. That is,a characteristic that take on different</p>\n<p>conditions e.g. attitude, skill, knowledge, interest, weather, time of day etc. In its</p>\n<p>specific research meaning, a variable refers to a characteristic of group of objects,</p>\n<p>people, events or situation which may change or differ.</p>\n<p>The most frequently encountered variables in research are independent variable,</p>\n<p>dependent variable, extraneous variable, moderator variable and control variable.</p>\n<p>a     Independent or treatment variable</p>\n<p>This is the variable that determines the nature and/or occurrence of another</p>\n<p>variable. Sometimes, this variable is called a predictor variable because it is</p>\n<p>responsible for the outcome a researcher get. For example a researcher may</p>\n<p>want to see the effect of interest on achievement in mathematics. The</p>\n<p>independent variable here is interest.</p>\n<p>b.          Dependent variable</p>\n<p>This is sometimes called criterion variable or the effect. This type depends</p>\n<p>on the treatment and can be manipulated. In the example above,</p>\n<p>achievement scores in mathematics are related to the independent variable</p>\n<p>(interest).</p>\n<p>c.          Extraneous (intervening variable)</p>\n<p>This is a variable which may or may not be known, but which in some way</p>\n<p>affects the outcome of a research study. For example fatigue, hunger or time</p>\n<p>of the day may affect the outcome of a lesson or text.</p>\n<p>d.          Moderator</p>\n<p>These are secondary independent variables which may not have been rtaken</p>\n<p>into consideration in a research study. Again, using the example of</p>\n<p>achievement in mathematics, it is possible that difference in gender, aptitude</p>\n<p>or socio-economic background of the subject might affect subjects’</p>\n<p>achievement in mathematics.</p>\n<p>Classification Based on Methods of Data Analysis.</p>\n<h4 id=\"there-are-three-types-of-research-based-on-methods-of-data-analysis-these-are\">There are three types of research based on methods of data analysis. These are:</h4>\n<p>i.        Qualitative - non computational</p>\n<p>ii.       Quantitative - Computational</p>\n<p>iii.      Triangulation or multiple perspectives.</p>\n<p>Qualitative Research</p>\n<p>Qualitative research has to do with collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data by</p>\n<p>observing what people do and say. This type of research is less objective than</p>\n<p>quantitative research and uses different methods of collecting information, mainly</p>\n<p>individual, in-depth interviews and focus group.</p>\n<p>Qualitative Research Types</p>\n<p>•   Phenomenology</p>\n<p>•   Ethnography</p>\n<p>•   Grounded theory</p>\n<p>•   Historical research</p>\n<p>•   Case study research</p>\n<p>Phenomenology – a form of qualitative research in which the researcher attempts to</p>\n<p>understand how one or more individuals experience a phenomenon. For example,</p>\n<p>you might interview 20 widowers and ask them to describe their experiences of the</p>\n<p>deaths of their wives.</p>\n<p>Ethnography – is the form of qualitative research that focuses on describing the</p>\n<p>culture of a group of people. Note that a culture is the shared attitudes, values,</p>\n<p>norms, practices, language, and material things of a group of people. For an</p>\n<p>example of ethnography, you might decide to go and live in Yoruba communities</p>\n<p>and study the culture and their educational practices.</p>\n<p>Grounded theory – is a qualitative approach to generating and developing a theory</p>\n<p>from data that the researcher collects. For an example, you might collect data from</p>\n<p>parents who have pulled their children out of public schools and develop a theory</p>\n<p>to explain how and why this phenomenon occurs, ultimately developing a theory</p>\n<p>of school pull-out.</p>\n<p>Historical research – This is research about events that occurred in the past. An</p>\n<p>example, you might study the use of corporal punishment in schools in the 19th</p>\n<p>century.</p>\n<p>Case study research – is a form of qualitative research that is focused on providing a</p>\n<p>detailed account of one or more cases. For an example, you might study a</p>\n<p>classroom that was given a new curriculum for technology use.</p>\n<p>Quantitative Research</p>\n<p>Quantitative research is research involving the use of structured questions where</p>\n<p>response options have been predetermined and a large number of respondents are</p>\n<p>involved. In this type of research, measurement must be objective, quantitative and</p>\n<p>statistically valid. It is all about numbers and objective hard data.</p>\n<p>Common differences usually cited between these two types of research include</p>\n<p>Qualitative                 Quantitative</p>\n<p>Subjective                  Objective</p>\n<p>Language emotions           Language numbers</p>\n<p>Method of investigation     Statistica surve like</p>\n<p>include focus group         l         y      Liker</p>\n<p>discussions, one-on-one     multiple choice, t</p>\n<p>interviews,      write-     scale</p>\n<p>in             response,    etc.</p>\n<p>bibliographic studies,</p>\n<p>case studies</p>\n<p>Research questions only     Developed             from</p>\n<p>research questions, null</p>\n<p>hypothesis may be</p>\n<p>statistically tested</p>\n<p>No statistical test         Statistical tests possible</p>\n<p>Exact replication not       Replication possible</p>\n<p>possibl</p>\n<p>e</p>\n<p>Potential                   Potential</p>\n<p>dependabilit                dependabilit</p>\n<p>y varies but usually low    y varies but usually high</p>\n<p>Literature review may       Literature review must</p>\n<p>be done as study            be done early in the</p>\n<p>progresses             or   study</p>\n<p>afterwards</p>\n<p>Triangulation or multiple perspectives (Mixed Methodology)</p>\n<p>This involves the merging of qualitative and quantitative methodologies.</p>\n<p>Triangulation is the term used to describe the combination of several qualitative</p>\n<p>methods or combining qualitative with quantitative methods. Four strategies for</p>\n<p>combining methodologies are common. These are;</p>\n<p>Qualitative and quantitative studies are conducted simultaneously.</p>\n<p>A qualitative study can be ongoing while multiple waves of quantitative studies are</p>\n<p>done</p>\n<p>A qualitative study can precede a quantitative</p>\n<p>study. A quantitative study can precede a</p>\n<p>qualitative study.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-research-procedure-or-techniques\">THE RESEARCH PROCEDURE OR TECHNIQUES</h2>\n<p>The research procedure is nothing other than a sequence of activities which when</p>\n<p>followed, will enable the investigator to achieve his aim.The following sequence or</p>\n<p>step is recommended if one is to embark upon a programme of research. These</p>\n<h4 id=\"basic-steps-include\">basic steps include:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-finding-a-suitable-research-topic-or-identifying-problem\">1.     Finding a suitable research topic or identifying problem,</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-defining-the-problem-clearly\">2.     Defining the problem clearly,</h3>\n<p>3      Reviewing available relevant literature to the problem.</p>\n<p>4      Formulating hypotheses</p>\n<p>5      Defining the population and selecting sample.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-designing-and-validating-the-instruments-for-data-collection\">6.     Designing and validating the instruments for data collection</h3>\n<p>7      Collecting the data</p>\n<p>8      Organization of data.</p>\n<p>9      Analysis of data</p>\n<p>10     Interpretation of data</p>\n<p>11     Conclusions</p>\n<h3 id=\"12-writing-the-report\">12.    Writing the report.</h3>\n<p>The Research Problem</p>\n<p>There is no doubt that finding a suitable research topic or research problem</p>\n<p>requires a lot of guidance and deep thinking. The choice of an area for research is a</p>\n<p>personal decision, depending mainly on the interest and attitude of the researcher.</p>\n<p>For students to be able to locate and choose suitable research topic the following</p>\n<p>guides may be used.</p>\n<p>a)     Personal motivation and interest in the study.</p>\n<p>b)     Usefulness to the society.</p>\n<p>c)     Contribution to knowledge.</p>\n<p>d)     Time available for the study.</p>\n<p>Ideas for a good research topic may come from personal observation and</p>\n<p>experience or from review of literature, and the choice of topic depend largely on</p>\n<p>interest and availability of facilities for the study.</p>\n<p>Sources of Research Problem</p>\n<p>Research problem can be found from a number of sources such as</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-unanswered-questions-past-researcher-may-generate-some-questions-which\">1.     Unanswered questions: Past researcher may generate some questions which</h3>\n<p>he fails to answer in his research.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-literature-review-in-reviewing-literature-examine-suggestions-for-further\">2.     Literature Review: In reviewing literature, examine suggestions for further</h3>\n<p>researches at the end of dissertations, in critique of other people research especially</p>\n<p>in review.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-unsatisfactory-state-of-affairs-if-people-feel-unsatisfied-with-the-way-things\">3.     Unsatisfactory state of affairs: If people feel unsatisfied with the way things</h3>\n<p>are it is an indication that something is wrong, which has to be rectified. This may</p>\n<p>be source of research problem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-missing-link-a-gap-in-thought-process-or-practice-e-g-what-motivates-students\">4.     Missing link: A gap in thought process or practice e.g. what motivates students?</h3>\n<h3 id=\"5-replication-of-study-repeating-a-research-within-the-same-context-after-a\">5.     Replication of study: Repeating a research within the same context after a</h3>\n<p>period of time or a study repeated under different geographical location.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-quasi-replication-previous-topic-repeated-with-alteration-to-some-part-of-the\">6.     Quasi-replication – previous topic repeated with alteration to some part of the</h3>\n<p>design</p>\n<h3 id=\"7-personal-experience-through-careful-observation-of-practice-in-the-area-of\">7.     Personal experience: Through careful observation of practice in the area of</h3>\n<p>one’s</p>\n<p>interest, a researcher can identified researchable problems.</p>\n<h3 id=\"8-from-existing-theories-most-theories-are-good-sources-of-researchable\">8.    From Existing Theories: Most theories are good sources of researchable</h3>\n<p>problem because theories normally give rise to deduction, which need to be tested</p>\n<p>through the instrumentality of research.</p>\n<h3 id=\"9-original-idea-develop-a-truly-researchable-topic-after-extensive-literature-review\">9.        Original idea – develop a truly researchable topic after extensive literature review</h3>\n<h3 id=\"10-recommendations-from-your-professor-or-advisor\">10.       Recommendations from your professor or advisor</h3>\n<p>Research Topics to be avoided</p>\n<p>-   Avoid philosophical topics or those based on personal belief</p>\n<p>-   Avoid strictly bibliographic topics that are already discussed fully in</p>\n<p>book-length studied</p>\n<p>-   Avoid describing processes. Such information will not allow for original</p>\n<p>insight and judgment on your part.</p>\n<p>-   Avoid topics too narrow or too recent for discussion. It will not allow for</p>\n<p>adequate use of research materials.</p>\n<p>-   Avoid philosophical topics or those based on personal belief</p>\n<p>-   Avoid strictly bibliographic topics that are already discussed fully in</p>\n<p>book-length studied</p>\n<p>-   Avoid describing processes. Such information will not allow for original</p>\n<p>insight and judgment on your part.</p>\n<p>-   Avoid topics too narrow or too recent for discussion. It will not allow for</p>\n<p>adequate use of research materials.</p>\n<p>-   Topics that are too big</p>\n<p>-   Topics that can be traced to a single source.</p>\n<p>-   Topics that is trivial – of small value</p>\n<p>-   Topics that are overused (over researched)</p>\n<h2 id=\"review-of-available-relevant-literature\">REVIEW OF AVAILABLE RELEVANT LITERATURE</h2>\n<p>A major step in conducting research is literature review. After selecting a topic one</p>\n<p>should make a thorough survey of what had already been known in the area of</p>\n<p>interest. Literature review is an examination and gathering of valuable topical and</p>\n<p>pertinent information already expressed from sources on issues of study which</p>\n<p>tend to modify and add weight to your study. It implies knowledge which is</p>\n<p>relevant and can buttress as well as negate the arguments of study in order to make</p>\n<p>contribution to your field of study.</p>\n<p>Also, it is through a review of previous work and findings in the area chosen that</p>\n<p>enable the researcher to develop his owns theoretical or conceptual framework</p>\n<p>upon which hypotheses</p>\n<p>are generated.</p>\n<p>The sources of literature to be reviewed on any problem include Journals, research</p>\n<p>review, encyclopedia, textbook, monographs, periodicals, research report,</p>\n<p>newspaper, bulleting, theses, dissertation, seminar paper etc.</p>\n<p>The presentation of literature review should be systematic and should cover all the</p>\n<p>variables involve in the study. A brief summary of the literature reviewed should be</p>\n<p>included in this aspect of the research report.</p>\n<p>Roles of Literature Review</p>\n<p>a.     A thorough knowledge of related research work enables the</p>\n<p>investigator to defined frontiers (boundaries).</p>\n<p>b.     Literature review enables researchers to place their questions in the</p>\n<p>right perspective.</p>\n<p>c.     By studying related research, the investigator learn which procedure</p>\n<p>and instruments have proved useful and which ones seem less promising</p>\n<p>and had to be improved upon;</p>\n<p>d.     A thorough search through previous research works makes an</p>\n<p>investigator to avoid unintentional replication of previous studies.</p>\n<p>e.     The study of related literature places a researcher in a better position</p>\n<p>to interpret the significance of their own result.</p>\n<p>f.     Literature review helps in surveying what is known and what is not</p>\n<p>yet known;</p>\n<p>g.     Literature review helps to identify previous research procedure</p>\n<p>instruments used, utility of the instruments, how to adapt or adopt the</p>\n<p>instruments, research design and hypotheses investigated.</p>\n<p>h.     Literature review helps to interpret findings and integrate them into</p>\n<p>the main body of knowledge.</p>\n<p>Procedure for Literature Review</p>\n<p>a. Identify appropriate literature to be thoroughly studied;</p>\n<p>b. Read the abstract or summary section of the literature to determine</p>\n<p>relevance and appropriateness;</p>\n<p>c. Skim the report to determine relevance before going on its development.</p>\n<p>Do not</p>\n<p>waste your time reviewing irrelevant literature.</p>\n<p>d. Begin with most recent studies in your area of interest/study and</p>\n<p>then work backward through earlier studies</p>\n<p>e. Read-widely in the selected textbooks/ journals, manuals, archives-letters,</p>\n<p>minutes, diaries, biographies, autobiographies monographs, etc.</p>\n<p>f. Be sure you indicate which parts of the notes are direct quotations from</p>\n<p>the author, and which ones are your own paraphrases.</p>\n<p>g. Literature review should be thorough and complete. A researcher should</p>\n<p>not rush research until after a thorough review of literature.</p>\n<p>i. Your work should highlight relevance of the findings in the previous studies,</p>\n<p>gap of knowledge to be contributed by your own study.</p>\n<p>k. Avoid replication of work done as discovered in the literature review,</p>\n<p>Research Objectives</p>\n<p>Objectives are statement of intent, a statement which indicates what the researcher</p>\n<p>aim to achieve at the end of the research. Objectives must always be set after</p>\n<p>having formulated a good research question. After all, they are to explain the way</p>\n<p>in which such question is going to be answered.</p>\n<p>Research is an organized investigation of a problem in which there is an attempt to</p>\n<p>gain solution to problem.</p>\n<p>To get right solution of a problem, clearly defined objectives are very important.</p>\n<p>Clearly defined objectives enlighten the way in which the researcher has to</p>\n<p>proceed. A research objective is a clear, concise declarative statement, which</p>\n<p>provides direction to investigate the variables.</p>\n<p>Generally research objectives focus on the way to measure the variables, such as to</p>\n<p>identify or describe them. Sometimes objectives are directed towards identifying</p>\n<p>the relationship or differences between two variables. Research objectives are the</p>\n<p>results sought by the researcher at the end of the research. It summarizes what is to</p>\n<p>be achieved by the study. Objective should therefore be closely related to the</p>\n<p>statement of the problem.</p>\n<p>Objectives help the researcher to be focused on the study to avoid the collection of</p>\n<p>unnecessary data and as well as organize the study in clearly defined parts or</p>\n<p>phases. Clearly</p>\n<p>defined objectives will facilitate the development of research methodology and will</p>\n<p>help to orient the collection, analysis, interpretation and utilization of data.</p>\n<p>Objectives are usually headed by active verbs such as: to identify, to establish to</p>\n<p>describe to determine, to estimate, to develop, to compare, to analyze etc.</p>\n<p>For example, the research objectives for a study of rate of unemployment in a</p>\n<h4 id=\"particular-region-may-be\">particular region may be:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-to-compare-the-unemployment-rate-among-all-countries-in-the-region\">1. To compare the unemployment rate among all countries in the region.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-to-analyze-the-unemployment-rate-evolution-from-2009-to-2019\">2. To analyze the unemployment rate evolution from 2009 to 2019.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-to-identify-the-factors-associated-with-high-unemployment-rates\">3. To identify the factors associated with high unemployment rates.</h3>\n<p>An objective is a clear statement of something that needs to be accomplished over a</p>\n<h4 id=\"period-of-time-and-therefore-must-be-smart-smart-objectives-are\">period of time and therefore must be SMART. SMART objectives are:</h4>\n<p>Specific – states exactly what you need to achieve</p>\n<p>Measurable – includes a quality or quantity measure</p>\n<p>Attainable– must be achieved</p>\n<p>Realistic – can be challenging but must be practicable and credible</p>\n<p>Time bound – with a clear end date or timescale. For example, to increase</p>\n<p>Bugema University enrolment by 10% is not time bound, while to increase Bugema</p>\n<p>University enrolment by 10% over the next six month is time bound.</p>\n<p>Research Questions</p>\n<p>Developing a good research question is one of the first critical steps in the research</p>\n<p>process. The research question, when appropriately written, will guide the research</p>\n<p>project and assist in the construction of a logical argument. The research question</p>\n<p>should be a clear, focused question that summarizes the issue that the researcher</p>\n<p>will investigate. Research questions are to be based on the objectives of the study.</p>\n<h4 id=\"how-to-develop-a-good-research-question\">How to Develop a Good Research Question:</h4>\n<p>•   Researchers should begin by identifying a broader subject of interest that lends</p>\n<p>itself</p>\n<p>to investigation. For example, a researcher may be interested in unemployment</p>\n<p>•   The next step is to do preliminary research on the general topic to find out</p>\n<p>what research has already been done and what literature already exists. How</p>\n<p>much research has been done on unemployment?</p>\n<p>•   Then begin to narrow the topic by asking open-ended &quot;how&quot; and &quot;why&quot;</p>\n<p>questions. For example, a researcher may want to consider the factors that</p>\n<p>are contributing to unemployment. Create a list of potential questions for</p>\n<p>consideration and choose one that interests you and provides an</p>\n<p>opportunity for exploration.</p>\n<h4 id=\"finally-evaluate-the-question-by-using-the-following-list-of-guidelines\">•   Finally, evaluate the question by using the following list of guidelines:</h4>\n<p>-   Is the research question one that is of interest to the researcher?</p>\n<p>-   Is the research question researchable? Consider the available</p>\n<p>time frame and the required resources. Is the methodology to</p>\n<p>conduct the research feasible?</p>\n<p>-   Is the research question measureable and will the process</p>\n<p>produce data that can be supported or contradicted?</p>\n<p>-   Is the research question too broad or too narrow?</p>\n<p>The following research questions could be derived from the objectives stated above.</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-is-there-any-difference-in-the-unemployment-rate-among-countries-in-the-region\">1. Is there any difference in the unemployment rate among countries in the region?</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-what-is-the-unemployment-rate-from-2009-to-2019\">2. What is the unemployment rate from 2009 to 2019?</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-what-are-the-factors-associated-with-high-unemployment-rates\">3. What are the factors associated with high unemployment rates?</h3>\n<p>Formulating Hypotheses</p>\n<p>Hypothesis could be defined as a prediction of the outcome of a research problem.</p>\n<p>It is a suggested answer to the research problem. It is always put in a declarative</p>\n<p>statement to relate the variables to each other generally or specifically.</p>\n<h4 id=\"two-types-of-hypotheses-are\">Two types of hypotheses are:</h4>\n<p>Null hypothesis</p>\n<p>Positive</p>\n<p>hypothesis</p>\n<p>Null hypothesis is stated in the negative form e.g. there is no significant relationship</p>\n<p>between</p>\n<p>sex and performance in mathematics.</p>\n<p>The positive (alternative) hypothesis is stated positively e.g. there is a significant</p>\n<p>relationship between sex and performance in mathematics.</p>\n<p>Alternative hypotheses could be directional or non-directional</p>\n<p>It is not in all research that we formulate hypotheses in some we have only</p>\n<p>research questions.</p>\n<p>Attributes of good Research Hypotheses</p>\n<p>-   Hypotheses should be correctly, clearly and precisely stated.</p>\n<p>-   Hypotheses should be testable and measurable.</p>\n<p>-   Hypotheses should state relationships between variables.</p>\n<p>-   Hypotheses should be limited in scope.</p>\n<p>-   Hypotheses should be stated as far as possible in simple terms</p>\n<p>Theoretical and Conceptual Framework</p>\n<p>Some researchers use both the theoretical and conceptual framework; it is advisable</p>\n<p>to use only one. They both provide clear explanations regarding the relationships</p>\n<p>of the variables</p>\n<p>Theoretical Framework</p>\n<p>This involves presentation of different theories showing the</p>\n<p>generalizations already expressed in relation to the problem.</p>\n<p>Conceptual Framework</p>\n<p>Conceptual framework is a detailed presentation of the variables to be observed in a</p>\n<p>study. Topic: Challenges in the Implementation of Inclusive Education.</p>\n<p>Conceptual Framework</p>\n<p>Inclusive Education Programme</p>\n<p>- Teaching learning resources</p>\n<p>-Friendliness of learners’ environment</p>\n<p>- Human capital</p>\n<p>- Efficacy of the Administration</p>\n<p>Figure 1: Variables Associated with the Study</p>\n<p>Figure 1: Variables Associated with the Study</p>\n<p>Operational Definition of Terms</p>\n<p>Topic: Influence of Selected Personal factors on Students’ Academic Performance</p>\n<p>Independent Variables                                              Dependent Variable</p>\n<p>Personal Factors</p>\n<p>Academic</p>\n<p>-     Gender                                                           Performance</p>\n<p>-     Students’ Attitude</p>\n<p>-      Socio Economic Status</p>\n<p>-      Truancy</p>\n<p>Topic:Comparative Analysis of the Effect of School Type on Secondary</p>\n<p>School</p>\n<p>Students’ Behavior and Academic Performance</p>\n<p>Conceptual Framework</p>\n<p>Independent Variables                  Dependent Variable</p>\n<p>Type of school</p>\n<p>Students’</p>\n<p>Single sex male                                                   Academic</p>\n<p>Behavior</p>\n<p>Single sex female                                                 Performance</p>\n<p>Co-educational</p>\n<p>Sample       and     Sampling</p>\n<p>Technique Defining the Population and Selecting</p>\n<p>Samples</p>\n<p>A population is defined as &quot;all members of any well-defined class of people, events</p>\n<p>of objects&#x27;&quot;. The first step in selecting the number required from the group of the</p>\n<p>people or objects is to define the population. This enable the researcher to</p>\n<p>establish clear boundaries of the conditions which specifies who shall be included</p>\n<p>or excluded from the study.</p>\n<p>Selecting Samples</p>\n<p>A sample is a portion of a population. To study a population, it is often necessary</p>\n<p>to take a representative sample. The sample means selecting some members of the</p>\n<p>entire population for use in the study. The main reason for studying a sample is to</p>\n<p>be able to make generalized statements about the entire population. To study the</p>\n<p>entire population may be difficult, time consuming and of course very costly,</p>\n<p>hence sample takes a fair portion as representative of the entire population.</p>\n<p>There are certain generally accepted methods of sampling which may be used. These</p>\n<h4 id=\"are\">are:</h4>\n<p>Random sampling (probability sampling)</p>\n<p>a. Simple random sampling (Balloting or Dip-hand method),</p>\n<p>b. Systematic sampling.</p>\n<p>c. Stratified random sampling.</p>\n<p>Non-probability sampling</p>\n<p>a. Purposive sampling.</p>\n<p>b. Cluster sampling</p>\n<p>c. Captive sampling.</p>\n<p>d. Convenience sampling.</p>\n<p>e. Voluntary sampling.</p>\n<p>f. Snow ball sampling.</p>\n<p>g. Event sampling.</p>\n<p>h. Time sampling.</p>\n<p>Simple Random Sampling</p>\n<p>This is the best known of the sampling procedures. The basis characteristic is that</p>\n<p>all members of the population have an equal chance of being included in the</p>\n<p>sample. For example if a population consists of 300 members the chance of</p>\n<p>selecting each member is 1/300.</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-steps-in-simple-random-sampling-are\">The steps in simple random sampling are:</h4>\n<p>i     Define the population.</p>\n<p>ii    List all members of the population.</p>\n<p>iii   Select the samples by employing a procedure where sheer chances determine</p>\n<p>which</p>\n<p>member on the list are drawn from the population.</p>\n<p>Simple random sampling can be carried out by balloting, card reshuffling, and use</p>\n<p>of table of random digit.</p>\n<p>Balloting: in balloting name or number is assigned to each element in the study by</p>\n<p>writing the name or number on pieces of paper, squeeze the paper and start</p>\n<p>picking it one after the other until the researcher have picked enough number he</p>\n<p>wishes to use as sample.</p>\n<p>Card reshuffling: like balloting name and number is assigned to each element and</p>\n<p>then the name or number is writing on each card to represent an element in the</p>\n<p>study. These cards are put together and shuffled. At intervals or at each time the</p>\n<p>cards are shuffled the researcher picks the card on top until he has picked enough</p>\n<p>to make up the sample.</p>\n<p>Use of Table of Random Digits: This involves numbering all the elements in the</p>\n<p>population. Then select digits from the table in any systematic way and the element</p>\n<p>whose number coincides with the random digits are included in the sample this</p>\n<p>process is continued until the desired sample size is reached.</p>\n<p>Systematic Random Sampling</p>\n<p>This involves collecting the list of the study population and then choose sample in</p>\n<p>a systematic way. This procedure involves drawing a sample by taking every Kth</p>\n<p>case from a population. Systematic sampling is also referred to as interval sampling.</p>\n<p>For example: if a sample of 50 students were to be selected from a departmental</p>\n<p>list with 500 names, the list of students in alphabetical order will be taken and</p>\n<p>every 10th name will be chosen as an elements in the sample.</p>\n<p>Systematic sampling differs from simple random sampling in that various choices</p>\n<p>are not dependent. Once the first case is chosen, all subsequent cases to be</p>\n<p>included in the sample are automatically determined.</p>\n<p>Stratified Random Sampling</p>\n<p>When the population consists of a number of sub group or strata that may differ in</p>\n<p>the characteristics being studied, it is often desirable to use a form of sampling</p>\n<p>technique called stratified   random    sampling.   This    procedure    can   be</p>\n<p>divided into proportionate and</p>\n<p>disproportionate. In proportionate stratified random sampling, sample are pick</p>\n<p>according to the number of subject in each strata while in disproportionate the</p>\n<p>strata are not equally represented it is either over represented or under represented.</p>\n<p>Purposive) Sampling</p>\n<p>The deliberate selection technique is used when there are specific cases or</p>\n<p>members of the population with certain crucial traits to the study and the</p>\n<p>researcher must find a way of including them. Sample that best meet your</p>\n<p>objectives or your target group.</p>\n<p>Cluster Sampling</p>\n<p>The cluster sampling is fairly similar to the stratified random sampling technique</p>\n<p>Cluster sampling is otherwise known as multistage sampling in that the population</p>\n<p>is sub-divided into units and a random sampling of smaller units that is, it is a</p>\n<p>population that exist in cluster over a geographical area. The selection of individual</p>\n<p>case in the group may also employ simple random technique. This method is used</p>\n<p>when the population is very large and covers a large geographical area. For example</p>\n<p>a country can be divided into regions or states which constitute sampling unit</p>\n<p>called clusters.</p>\n<p>Captive Sampling: The captive sampling is employed when the researcher decide</p>\n<p>to use only the sample he can get. This is what television Reporters use in</p>\n<p>sampling public opinion on topical issues.</p>\n<p>Convenience sampling: Selecting those sample that are most convenient or those</p>\n<p>immediately available.</p>\n<p>Voluntary Sampling: Sample self-selecting. This is when individuals come</p>\n<p>forward voluntarily in response to an appeal from the researcher.</p>\n<p>Snow ball sampling: This has to do with building up a sample through</p>\n<p>informants. You start with one person who then suggests another and so on.</p>\n<p>Event Sampling: This involves using the opportunity presented by a particular</p>\n<p>event e.g. a conference for sampling.</p>\n<p>Time Sampling: This involves recognizing that different times or days of the</p>\n<p>week or year may be significant and sampling carried out at this period of the day</p>\n<h2 id=\"sample-size-sample-adequacy\">SAMPLE SIZE/ SAMPLE ADEQUACY</h2>\n<p>Sample size depend on research type</p>\n<p>Use of Sample percentages (Newman 2000)</p>\n<p>Descriptive research – at least 10% when the population is large and 20% or more</p>\n<p>when the population is small</p>\n<p>Correlational research – at least 30 subjects</p>\n<p>Experimental research – 30 subjects per group</p>\n<p>Experimental with tight experimental control – 15 subjects per</p>\n<p>group Causal comparative – at least 30 subjects per group</p>\n<p>Sample Size Formula</p>\n<p>Determination of sample size is one of the problems students face when writing</p>\n<p>research project. Determination of sample size precedes the construction of</p>\n<p>instruments. Miscalculation of sample size will lead to wrong result therefore the</p>\n<p>conclusion reached after analysis cannot be generalized to the entire population</p>\n<p>under study.</p>\n<p>Taro Yamane formula has been found to be most reliable and effective way of</p>\n<p>calculating sample size. Yamane was a mathematician and statistician who</p>\n<p>developed statistical formula for calculating sample size based on the population</p>\n<p>under study so that inferences and conclusion reached after survey can be</p>\n<p>generalized to the entire population.</p>\n<p>Sample size formula (Slovin, 1960; Taro Yamane,</p>\n<p>1967) n        =     N</p>\n<p>1 + N (e) 2</p>\n<p>n = Sample size</p>\n<p>N = Population Size</p>\n<p>e = desired margin of error (Usually 0.05 for Social researches)</p>\n<p>NB: Other sample size formulae available</p>\n<p>Use of Sample Size Table</p>\n<p>The use of sample size table such as developed by Krejaie &amp;Morgan (1970)</p>\n<h2 id=\"designing-and-validating-research-instruments\">DESIGNING AND VALIDATING RESEARCH INSTRUMENTS</h2>\n<h2 id=\"research-instruments-tools\">RESEARCH INSTRUMENTS (TOOLS)</h2>\n<h4 id=\"the-instruments-for-collecting-research-data-are\">The instruments for collecting research data are:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-observation\">1. Observation</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-interview\">2. Interview</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-document-analysis\">3. Document Analysis</h3>\n<h3 id=\"4-questionnaires-recording-schedule\">4. Questionnaires/recording schedule</h3>\n<h3 id=\"5-rating-scale-opinionnaire-attitude-scale\">5. Rating scale/opinionnaire/attitude scale</h3>\n<h3 id=\"6-psychological-test\">6. Psychological test</h3>\n<h3 id=\"7-inventory-self-reporting-scale\">7. Inventory/self- reporting scale.</h3>\n<p>The choice of research instrument depend on the nature of the study</p>\n<p>Direct Observation</p>\n<p>In the strict sense, observation means the use of the eyes rather than the</p>\n<p>ears and the voice. The concise oxford dictionary defines it as accurate watching</p>\n<p>and noting of phenomena as they occur with regard to the cause and effect or</p>\n<p>mutual relations. Observation when used in research gives a more reliable and</p>\n<p>valid results</p>\n<p>The accuracy of information can be improved by the following procedures.</p>\n<p>(a) Observation should be planned</p>\n<p>(b) A written record should be made directly after an observation</p>\n<p>(c) Interpretation should follow the collection of data</p>\n<p>Interview</p>\n<p>Interview is a process that involves at least two people in a face-to-face oral</p>\n<p>discussion with the aim of wanting to get information one from the other. The two</p>\n<p>people are interviewer and interviewee (candidate) respectively.</p>\n<p>There are two main types of interview in use viz: structured and</p>\n<p>unstructured</p>\n<p>interviews. Interview could also be conducted through the telephone.</p>\n<p>Structured interview is one in which the content and procedure of the</p>\n<p>interview are organized in advance of the interview. Questions and alternative</p>\n<p>answers permitted are pre- determined, arranged in logical sequence and rigidly</p>\n<p>followed with all respondents. Interviewer may however deviate from the set</p>\n<p>questions for case of clarification of points.</p>\n<p>The advantages of structured interview are that the approach is standardized</p>\n<p>and answer can easily be classified. Secondly, there is increased reliability as a result</p>\n<p>of restriction. It has disadvantage by the fact that the interviewee does not enjoy</p>\n<p>enough freedom to explain or expatiate on the responses made.</p>\n<p>In unstructured interview, there is no format. The interviewer does not</p>\n<p>need to follow a laid down pattern of asking questions. He can thus go deep in the</p>\n<p>questioning exercise. The candidates enjoy ample opportunity.</p>\n<p>The disadvantage of unstructured interview is that there is no sequence and</p>\n<p>vital questions may be forgotten. It does not lead itself readily to quantification.</p>\n<p>On mode of interview, it may be conducted on a one to one basis or on a</p>\n<p>group basis (focus group). Other purposes for which interview may be used are</p>\n<p>admission, employment, promotion, scholarship award and leadership roles.</p>\n<h4 id=\"precautions-to-be-taken-when-conducting-interviews-are\">Precautions to be taken when conducting interviews are:</h4>\n<p>- Generate friendly situation.</p>\n<p>- Do not argue with the interviewee.</p>\n<p>- Do not show signs of content or disagreement.</p>\n<p>- Give an accurate report devoid of bias.</p>\n<p>- Observe high degree of confidentiality about the information in respect of</p>\n<p>the interviewee.</p>\n<p>Document Analysis</p>\n<p>Document analysis is a form of qualitative research in which documents are</p>\n<p>interpreted by the researcher to give voice and meaning around an assessment</p>\n<p>topic. Analyzing documents incorporates coding content into themes similar to</p>\n<p>how focus group or interview transcripts are analyzed (Bowen, 2009).</p>\n<p>Advantages of Document Analysis</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-contain-spontaneous-data-and-therefore-less-time-consuming-it-requires-data\">1.   Contain spontaneous data and therefore less time consuming. It requires data</h3>\n<p>selection, instead of data collection.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-accessibility-to-information-that-would-be-difficult-to-get-in-any-other-way\">2.     Accessibility to information that would be difficult to get in any other way</h3>\n<p>i.e readily available.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-make-possible-the-collection-of-data-over-a-longer-period-of-time-as-well\">3.     Make possible the collection of data over a longer period of time as well</h3>\n<p>as larger samples that might be collected from questionnaires or interviews</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-confessional-documents-may-give-us-an-insight-into-how-people-see-things-or\">4.     Confessional documents may give us an insight into how people see things or</h3>\n<p>how they want to present things.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-stability-documents-are-non-reactive-and-stable-the-investigator-s-presence\">5.     Stability: Documents are non-reactive and stable. The investigator’s presence</h3>\n<p>does not</p>\n<p>alter what is being studied</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-exactness-the-inclusion-of-exact-names-references-and-details-of\">6.     Exactness: The inclusion of exact names, references, and details of</h3>\n<p>events makes documents advantageous in the research process.</p>\n<h3 id=\"7-relatively-low-cost-particularly-when-the-documents-are-easily-accessible-and\">7.     Relatively low cost, particularly when the documents are easily accessible and</h3>\n<p>already located in your workplace, or on the internet.</p>\n<h3 id=\"8-some-types-of-document-can-be-extremely-detailed-and-yield-much-more\">8.     Some types of document can be extremely detailed and yield much more</h3>\n<p>information than you could hope to gain from a questionnaire or interview.</p>\n<p>Disadvantages of Document Analysis</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-documents-are-usually-not-designed-with-research-in-mind\">1.     Documents are usually not designed with research in mind.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-the-information-recorded-may-be-idiosyncratic-bias-or-incomplete-can-be-too\">2.     The information recorded may be idiosyncratic, bias or incomplete/can be too</h3>\n<p>subjective.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-ethical-principles-may-be-contravened\">3.     Ethical principles may be contravened.</h3>\n<p>Criteria for Handling Documentary Sources</p>\n<p>The general principles for handling documentary sources are not different from</p>\n<p>those of other applied and social sciences researches. Scott (199) in Mogalakwe</p>\n<p>(2006) formulated quality control criteria for handling documentary sources. These</p>\n<h4 id=\"are\">are:</h4>\n<p>a.     Authenticity</p>\n<p>b.     Credibility</p>\n<p>c.     Representativeness and</p>\n<p>d.     Meaning</p>\n<p>Authenticity refers to whether the evidence is genuine, and of reliable and</p>\n<p>dependable origin. This is a fundamental criterion in any research. It is therefore</p>\n<p>the responsibility of the researcher to ensure that the document consulted is</p>\n<p>genuine and has integrity. There are many instances where documents may not be</p>\n<p>what they purport to be as documents such as wills, legal documents, diaries and</p>\n<p>letters can be forged or falsified. Some literary works may even</p>\n<p>be attributed to authors who did not write them.</p>\n<p>Documents should not be taken for granted thus placing enormous responsibility</p>\n<p>on researchers to satisfy themselves that the documents being analyzed are not</p>\n<p>forged and indeed what they purport to be. After establishing the authenticity of a</p>\n<p>document it is also important for the researcher to authenticate the authorship by</p>\n<p>verifying that the name inscribed on the document is that of the actual author.</p>\n<p>Credibility refers to whether the evidence is free from error or distortion. Credibility</p>\n<p>concern the extent to which an observer is sincere in the choice of a point of view</p>\n<p>in an attempt to record an accurate account from the chosen standpoint.</p>\n<p>Representativeness refers to whether the evidence is typical of its kind while</p>\n<p>meaningrefers to whether the evidence is clear and comprehensive. The ultimate</p>\n<p>purpose of examining documents is to arrive at an understanding of the meaning</p>\n<p>and significance of what the document contains.</p>\n<p>Questionnaires</p>\n<p>Questionnaire is a self-reporting system like the structured interview; but the</p>\n<p>responses given to questions are made in writing. Questionnaires are sometimes</p>\n<p>referred to as recording schedule.</p>\n<p>As compared with interviews, the written questionnaire is typically more</p>\n<p>efficient and practical and allows for the use of large sample, standard instructions</p>\n<p>are given to all subjects and the personal appearance; mood or conduct of the</p>\n<p>investigator will not color the results. There are two types of questionnaires namely</p>\n<p>structured (closed ended or form) and unstructured (open ended or form)</p>\n<p>Structured or closed form contains the questions and alternative answers.</p>\n<p>Unstructured questionnaire has the advantage of giving the respondent freedom to</p>\n<p>reveal their opinion and attitude. It has the disadvantage that the information</p>\n<p>generated is difficult to process and analyze.</p>\n<p>The following should be avoided while constructing questionnaires.</p>\n<p>- Avoid highbrow questions i.e. the use of high sound English to impress</p>\n<p>respondents. Use simple English.</p>\n<p>- Avoid leading questions</p>\n<p>- Avoid open ended questions or questions that are insufficiently specific.</p>\n<p>Advantages of Questionnaires</p>\n<p>- Can be used in the absence of the author.</p>\n<p>- Can be conducted to many people at the same time.</p>\n<p>- No psychological discomfort that may be experienced during face- to- face</p>\n<p>interaction.</p>\n<p>- Can be used to obtain reliable information if well-constructed.</p>\n<p>Disadvantages</p>\n<p>- Misinterpretation of questions.</p>\n<p>- Low return of questionnaires.</p>\n<p>Rating Scale</p>\n<p>Rating scale is a measuring device in which the assignment of numerals to</p>\n<p>the character of individual or behavior is made possible through the process of</p>\n<p>well-defined rules. The most widely used is the Likert scale for measuring</p>\n<p>attitude. The Likert scale is a five point level of agreement from strongly agree</p>\n<p>to strongly disagree. Thre are modified version of the scale today.</p>\n<h2 id=\"examples\">EXAMPLES</h2>\n<h2 id=\"a-5-4-3-2-1\">A                                                               5       4   3   2   1</h2>\n<h2 id=\"sa-a-un-d-sd\">SA A UN D SD</h2>\n<p>1    Jesus was born of a virgin without a human</p>\n<p>father</p>\n<p>2    There is a final day of judgment for all who</p>\n<p>have lived on</p>\n<p>earth</p>\n<p>B      How often and which section of the Times Newspaper do you read?</p>\n<p>Always Usually    Sometime Seldom Never</p>\n<p>s</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-state-and-local-news\">1. State and local news</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-sports\">2. Sports</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-advertising\">3. Advertising</h3>\n<p>C      How would you rate your Principal on the following traits?</p>\n<p>Excellent V.        Good Fair       Poor</p>\n<p>Good</p>\n<p>1    Competence</p>\n<p>2    Friendliness</p>\n<p>3   Helpful</p>\n<p>4   Ability to inspire</p>\n<p>Psychological Tests</p>\n<p>A psychological test is an instrument designed to describe and measure a</p>\n<p>sample of certain aspect of human behavior. Test may be used to compare the</p>\n<p>behavior of two or more persons at a particular time or of one person or more at</p>\n<p>different times.</p>\n<p>Psychological tests which are quantified by numerical scores yield objective</p>\n<p>and standardized description of behaviors. Psychological tests may be classified in</p>\n<p>terms of their purpose as achievement, aptitude, intelligence etc.</p>\n<p>Inventories (Self reporting scale)</p>\n<p>In inventories, the individual checks responses to certain questions or statements.</p>\n<p>Two main types are interest and personality inventories. Interest inventory is a</p>\n<p>measure of type of activities that an individual has a tendency to like and to choose</p>\n<p>while personality test measures certain personality traits or tendencies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"validation-of-research-instrument\">VALIDATION OF RESEARCH INSTRUMENT</h2>\n<p>It is necessary that research tools used are of very good quality. Two essential</p>\n<p>qualities of a good research instrument are validity and reliability.</p>\n<p>Validity</p>\n<p>The most important consideration is validity, which pertains to the question</p>\n<p>“what does this instrument measure?” Validity is the extent to which an instrument</p>\n<p>measures what it is supposed to measure. The face and content validity are</p>\n<p>commonly used in research.</p>\n<p>Face Validity.</p>\n<p>This refers to how reasonable the instrument appears as an instrument of</p>\n<p>the particular attribute intended. In other words, face validity is the extent to which</p>\n<p>one is able to accept on a mere superficial inspection of the instrument that it is</p>\n<p>actually an instrument of what it is intended to be. Face validity is not a serious</p>\n<p>form of validity.</p>\n<p>Content Validity</p>\n<p>This means the extent to which the content of the instrument correspond to</p>\n<p>those of the subject matter and their associated behavioral outcome. Content</p>\n<p>validity is measured by analyzing the topical contents and the concepts to be</p>\n<p>measured. The closer the instrument contents are to those of the subject matter</p>\n<p>the higher is the content validity of the instrument.</p>\n<p>Other forms of validity are criterion related validity, predictive validity,</p>\n<p>concurrent validity, congruent validity, constructs validity.</p>\n<p>Sources of Invalidity</p>\n<p>•   Unreliability</p>\n<p>•   Response sets - psychological orientation or bias towards answering in a</p>\n<h4 id=\"particular-way\">particular way:</h4>\n<p>Ø Acquiescence: tendency to agree, i.e. say &quot;Yes”.</p>\n<p>Ø Social desirability: tendency to portray self in a positive light.</p>\n<p>Ø Faking bad: The fake bad response style is defined as any conscious</p>\n<p>or unconscious attempt to produce item-- endorsement representing</p>\n<p>a more exaggerated symptomatology than is actually experienced by</p>\n<p>the respondent, with components of un-believability and an</p>\n<p>unrealistic negative impression.</p>\n<p>•   Bias</p>\n<p>Ø Cultural bias: When psychological construct do not have the same</p>\n<p>meaning based on culture. Actual content (face) validity may be</p>\n<p>different for different cultures.</p>\n<p>Ø Gender bias may also be possible.</p>\n<p>Ø Test Bias</p>\n<p>Reliability</p>\n<p>An instrument is said to be reliable if it measures consistently under varying</p>\n<p>conditions and at different times. Reliability is measured by means of statistics</p>\n<p>known as coefficient of reliability.</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-main-methods-of-assessing-reliability-are\">The main methods of assessing reliability are:</h4>\n<p>(i) Test- retest method.</p>\n<p>(ii) Equivalent- forms method.</p>\n<p>(iii) Split- half method</p>\n<p>(iv) Scorer reliability.</p>\n<p>Test- retest method.</p>\n<p>To estimate reliability by means of test- retest method, the same instrument</p>\n<p>is administered to the same set of respondents with a given time interval</p>\n<p>between the two</p>\n<p>administrations. The resulting scores are correlated, and this correlation coefficient</p>\n<p>provides a measure of stability that is it indicates how stable the instrument</p>\n<p>results are over the given period of time. Today, an instrument could be</p>\n<p>administered once, coded and reliability determined by means of computer using</p>\n<p>the Cronbach&#x27;s Alpha Reliability Method Equivalent- forms method</p>\n<p>Estimating reliability by means of the equivalent- forms methods involves</p>\n<p>the use of two different but equivalent forms of the instrument (also called parallel</p>\n<p>or alternate forms). The two forms are administered to the same group of</p>\n<p>respondents in close succession and the resulting scores are correlated. The</p>\n<p>correlation coefficient provides a measure of equivalence. Thus it indicates the</p>\n<p>degree to which both forms of the instrument are measuring the same aspect of</p>\n<p>behavior.</p>\n<p>Spilt- Half method</p>\n<p>The reliability of an instrument can be estimated from a single</p>\n<p>administration of the instrument. The instrument is administered to a group of</p>\n<p>respondents in the usual manner and then divides in half for scoring purposes. To</p>\n<p>split the instrument in halves that are more equivalent, the usual procedure is to</p>\n<p>score the even-numbered items and the odd-numbered items separately. This</p>\n<p>provides two scores for each respondent, which when correlated provide a</p>\n<p>measure of internal consistency. This coefficient indicates the degree to which</p>\n<p>equivalent results are obtained from the two halves of the instrument.</p>\n<p>To estimate the reliability based on the full-length test, the Spearman-Brown</p>\n<h4 id=\"formula-is-usually-applied-this-formula-is-given-as\">formula is usually applied. This formula is given as:</h4>\n<p>Reliability of full test = 2 x r</p>\n<p>1+r</p>\n<p>Where r = Reliability of half test.</p>\n<p>The simplicity of the formula can be seen in the following example where the</p>\n<p>correlation coefficient between the two halves of a test is 0.60</p>\n<p>Reliability of = 2 x 0.60          = 1.20</p>\n<p>1 + 0.60   1.60</p>\n<p>= 0.75</p>\n<p>Kuder- Richardson Method</p>\n<p>Another method of estimating the reliability of a test from a single administration</p>\n<p>is by means of formulae such as those developed by Kuder and Richardson. These</p>\n<p>formulae provide a measure of internal consistency but they do not require</p>\n<p>splitting the test in half for scoring purposes.</p>\n<p>A simpler formula for this purpose is the Kuder- Richardson formula 21 which can</p>\n<p>be applied to the results of any test that has been scored on the basis of the</p>\n<p>number of correct answers. This method is only suitable for dichotomized items</p>\n<p>e.g Yes/No, Agree/Disagree</p>\n<p>Modified version of the</p>\n<p>formula Reliability estimate</p>\n<h2 id=\"kr-21\">(KR 21)</h2>\n<h2 id=\"k-1-m-k-m\">= K (1 - M (K-M</h2>\n<h2 id=\"k-1-ks2\">) K-1    KS2</h2>\n<p>Where</p>\n<p>K= the number of items in the</p>\n<p>test. M= the mean of the test</p>\n<p>scores.</p>\n<p>S= the standard deviation of the test scores.</p>\n<p>Scorer Reliability</p>\n<p>This measures the extent to which different markers (inter-scorer) or the</p>\n<p>same marker (intra scorer) on different occasions will award the same score to the</p>\n<p>same answer. The marks awarded on different occasions by different markers or</p>\n<p>the same marker to the same set of testees is correlated to obtain a measure of</p>\n<p>scorer reliability.</p>\n<h2 id=\"research-data-analysis\">RESEARCH DATA ANALYSIS</h2>\n<p>Research data analysis is the most important aspect of research. The issue of</p>\n<p>statistical data analysis is more of a specialized field, but students carrying out</p>\n<p>research need at least a rudimentary aspect of data analysis. Following are hints on</p>\n<p>qualitative data analysis, quantitative data analysis and the use of computer in data</p>\n<p>analysis.</p>\n<p>Qualitative Data Analysis</p>\n<p>In qualitative research, the focus is on in-depth interviews, observations and</p>\n<p>document analysis. In general, qualitative research interprets data without</p>\n<p>numerical   analysis,   however   some    qualitative   research   include   limited</p>\n<p>quantification e.g counting the number of occurrences of an event.</p>\n<p>There are three processes in qualitative data analysis namely data organization,</p>\n<p>data description and data interpretation. The first which is data organization is</p>\n<p>critical due to the often voluminous data collected in quantitative studies. The</p>\n<p>second step is the description of the data, the setting in which they were collected</p>\n<p>and the participants in the study. Finally, the data must be interpreted.</p>\n<p>Quantitative Data Analysis</p>\n<p>Quantitative research requires statistical testing. There are four major</p>\n<p>statistical testing that could be used by students in their data analysis. These are:</p>\n<p>i.        Test of Significance</p>\n<p>ii.       Test of central tendency</p>\n<p>iii.      Test of variation and</p>\n<p>iv.       Parametric and non-parametric tests</p>\n<p>In addition to using the statistical methods above, the researcher could use</p>\n<p>charts, graph, diagrams, models and other figures to drive home their points to</p>\n<p>provide clear understanding of their solution to the problem. For orderliness and</p>\n<p>systematic solution of the research problem, the research hypothesis or questions</p>\n<p>could be tackled one after the other and appropriate test used for each of them</p>\n<p>Computer Data Analysis</p>\n<p>The electronic digital computer is one of the most versatile developments of</p>\n<p>the technological age. To the researcher, the use of computer to analyze complex</p>\n<p>data has made complicated research design practical. Performing calculations</p>\n<p>almost at the speed of light, the computer has become one of the most useful tools</p>\n<p>in the physical and behavioral sciences as well as humanities.</p>\n<p>The steps in using computer to calculate statistical analysis are;</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-data-organizing-and-coding\">1. Data organizing and coding</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-storing-the-data-in-the-computer\">2. Storing the data in the computer</h3>\n<h3 id=\"3-labeling-the-variables\">3. Labeling the variables</h3>\n<h3 id=\"4-selecting-of-appropriate-descriptive-and-inferential-statistics\">4. Selecting of appropriate descriptive and inferential statistics</h3>\n<h3 id=\"5-selection-of-appropriate-programmes-for-described-statistics\">5. Selection of appropriate programmes for described statistics</h3>\n<h3 id=\"6-execution-of-the-computer\">6. Execution of the computer</h3>\n<p>programme Two main statistical</p>\n<p>packages are</p>\n<p>SPSS – Statistical Packages for Social Sciences</p>\n<p>SAS – Statistical Analysis System</p>\n<h2 id=\"ethical-considerations-in-research\">ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS IN RESEARCH</h2>\n<p>Informed Consent. Potential research participants must be provided with</p>\n<p>information that enables them to make an informed decision as to whether</p>\n<p>they want to participate in the research study</p>\n<p>Informed Consent from parents of Participants. Informed consent must be</p>\n<p>obtained from parents or guardians of minors.</p>\n<p>Do not withhold information from participants about the nature and/or</p>\n<p>purpose of the study</p>\n<p>Freedom to Withdraw: Participants must be informed that they are free to</p>\n<p>withdraw from the study at any time without penalty.</p>\n<p>Confidentiality and Anonymity</p>\n<p>Confidentiality is a basic requirement in all studies. It means that the</p>\n<p>researcher agrees not to reveal the identity of the participant to anyone other</p>\n<p>than the researcher and his or her staff.</p>\n<p>A stronger and even better condition (if it can be met) is called anonymity.</p>\n<p>Anonymity means that the identity of the participant is not known by anyone in</p>\n<p>the study, including the researcher. An example would be where the researcher</p>\n<p>has a large group of people fill out a questionnaire but NOT write their names</p>\n<p>on it. In this way, the researcher ends up with data, but no names</p>\n<h2 id=\"research-proposal\">RESEARCH PROPOSAL</h2>\n<p>Framework of the Research Proposal</p>\n<p>A research proposal is a written document that includes the following information:</p>\n<p>-   Introduction</p>\n<p>-   Literature Review</p>\n<p>-   Research Methodology</p>\n<p>The introduction part introduces your research idea, establish its importance and</p>\n<p>explain its significance. The literature review is an exploration of what other</p>\n<p>researchers have done in the area of study, while the research methodology explain</p>\n<p>in details how the research will be conducted. It provides a written description of</p>\n<p>the specific actions, plan, or strategy you will take to answer your research</p>\n<p>questions.</p>\n<p>The purpose of your proposal is to sell your idea by showing you have thought it</p>\n<p>through</p>\n<p>very carefully and have planned a good research study.</p>\n<p>The research proposal can serve many useful functions.</p>\n<p>•     The most important is that it helps you to think out the research project you</p>\n<p>are about to undertake and predict any difficulties that might arise.</p>\n<p>•     For those who are not sure of what their focus will be, the research proposal</p>\n<p>can be a space to explore options -- perhaps with one proposal for each</p>\n<p>potential topic. These can be more easily compared and evaluated than</p>\n<p>when they are still just ideas in one&#x27;s head.</p>\n<p>•     Research proposals can be effective starting points to discuss projects with</p>\n<p>your professors.</p>\n<p>•     A professor who is initially skeptical about a project may be able to imagine</p>\n<p>it more easily after reading a well written research proposal (this does not</p>\n<p>mean that he or she will approve the topic, especially if there are significant</p>\n<p>potential difficulties that you have not considered).</p>\n<p>•     Once you have begun your research project, a research proposal can help</p>\n<p>you to remain on track -- and can also remind you why you started this</p>\n<p>project in the first place</p>\n<p>The format for the writing a research proposal may vary from one department to</p>\n<p>another and as a result research students need to check with the department.</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-the-final-research-report\">WRITING THE FINAL RESEARCH REPORT</h2>\n<p>A good research with many meaningful findings may lose its value and be thrown</p>\n<p>into the dust-bin if the writing of the research report is badly done. A good</p>\n<p>research report writing entails hard work and patience.</p>\n<p>Researchers can adopt any format for writing the report but should, essentially</p>\n<p>reflect the research process. A sample of an outline of search report is presented</p>\n<h4 id=\"below\">below:</h4>\n<p>Preliminary Pages</p>\n<p>Title Page</p>\n<p>Certification</p>\n<p>Dedication</p>\n<p>Acknowledgeme</p>\n<p>nt Table of</p>\n<p>Contents List of</p>\n<p>Tables</p>\n<p>List of Figures</p>\n<p>List of</p>\n<p>Appendices</p>\n<p>Abstract</p>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-one-introduction\">CHAPTER ONE: INTRODUCTION</h2>\n<p>Background to the</p>\n<p>Study Statement of the</p>\n<p>problem Purpose of the</p>\n<p>study Objectives of the</p>\n<p>Study</p>\n<p>Research</p>\n<p>Questions/Hypotheses</p>\n<p>Significance of the study</p>\n<p>Scope and Delimitations of the Study</p>\n<p>Conceptual/Theoretical Framework</p>\n<p>Operational Definitions of Terms</p>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-two-review-of-related\">CHAPTER TWO: REVIEW OF RELATED</h2>\n<h2 id=\"literature-chapter-iii-research\">LITERATURE CHAPTER III: RESEARCH</h2>\n<h2 id=\"methodology\">METHODOLOGY</h2>\n<p>Research Approach</p>\n<p>Research Design</p>\n<p>Population of the Study</p>\n<p>Sample and Sampling Technique</p>\n<p>Instrumentation</p>\n<p>Validation of</p>\n<p>Instrument Method of</p>\n<p>Data Collection Method</p>\n<p>of Data Analysis Ethical</p>\n<p>Consideration</p>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-four-data-analysis-and-interpretation\">CHAPTER FOUR: DATA ANALYSIS AND INTERPRETATION</h2>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-five-summary-conclusion-and-recommendation\">CHAPTER FIVE: SUMMARY, CONCLUSION AND RECOMMENDATION</h2>\n<h2 id=\"references\">REFERENCES</h2>\n<h2 id=\"appendices\">APPENDICES</h2>\n<h2 id=\"writing-research-reports\">WRITING RESEARCH REPORTS</h2>\n<h4 id=\"writing-the-main-report-of-a-study\">Writing the main report of a study:</h4>\n<p>One of the most important aspects of any research study is communication of the</p>\n<p>results to others. This important aspect can be accomplished through a well</p>\n<p>written report. Most reports start with the title. The title is the label given to the</p>\n<p>report. It should be written in such a way that the reader can easily tell what the</p>\n<p>study is all about, even without reading the whole report. The title should not be</p>\n<p>more than 20 substantive words. After the title comes the abstract. The Abstract</p>\n<p>should contain important ideas of the study. The abstract is a brief summary of the</p>\n<p>whole study containing main steps in the study, statement of purpose, the types of</p>\n<p>subjects, brief statement of the design, data analysis, result, and findings. Usually</p>\n<p>the abstract should not be more than 350 words.</p>\n<p>Chapter One: Introduction</p>\n<p>This chapter usually includes the introduction where the reader should be properly</p>\n<p>introduced to the problem. After the introduction comes the statement about the</p>\n<p>background to the study and other sub-heading as spelt out above</p>\n<p>Chapter Two: Review of Related Literature.</p>\n<p>Chapter two deals mainly with review of related literature highlighting previous</p>\n<p>research work in related studies. Review of previous studies should not be limited</p>\n<p>only to local studies. It should be international in scope.</p>\n<p>Chapter Three: Research Design and Procedure</p>\n<p>Here, the researcher should clearly explain step by step the nature of design used,</p>\n<p>the sample, the instrument, and the procedure of the study.</p>\n<p>Chapter Four: Data Analysis and Interpretation</p>\n<p>Data could be analyzed quantitatively or qualitatively. In quantitative data analysis,</p>\n<p>the important thing under presentation of results is that all tables, charts, graphs</p>\n<p>etc. should be clearly labeled and explained so that readers may understand the</p>\n<p>report. Results are to be presented in line with the research questions/hypothesis.</p>\n<h3 id=\"chapter-5-summary-conclusion-and-recommendation\">Chapter 5: Summary, Conclusion and Recommendation</h3>\n<p>In this chapter the researcher summarizes his research procedure, results and</p>\n<p>discussions; he then draws concrete conclusions from his findings and makes</p>\n<p>scholarly recommendation based on his findings.</p>\n<p>References, Appendices and Bibliography</p>\n<p>A. References</p>\n<p>The final state on reporting is referencing. References are those works or studies</p>\n<p>which the research cited in his report. This does not include all other works he</p>\n<p>consulted but did not cite. If however, the researcher wants to include the list of</p>\n<p>the works of studies he consulted but not cited in addition to the one cited, then</p>\n<h4 id=\"he-is-not-writing-a-reference-but-a-bibliography\">he is not writing a reference, but a bibliography.    :</h4>\n<p>Usually references and bibliographies are listed in alphabetical order. First the</p>\n<p>authors family name, initials, year and title of the work. If the cited work is a</p>\n<p>journal, the journal name is underlined, followed by the volume, number and</p>\n<p>pages. If the cited work is a book or other materials, the title is underlined,</p>\n<p>followed by the country/city of publication and publishers. References should be</p>\n<p>indicated clearly enough for the reader to get at it if he so wishes.</p>\n<h4 id=\"three-commonly-used-referencing-styles-are\">Three commonly used referencing styles are:</h4>\n<p>American Psychological Association (APA) – Education and Social</p>\n<p>Sciences Modern Language Association (MLA) - Languages</p>\n<p>Kate Turabian Style – Theology.</p>\n<p>NOTE: For details and guidance, search for APA referencing style online.</p>\n<p>The APA Referencing Style (Sample)</p>\n<p>Books (One author)</p>\n<p>Kmoisar, L. (1991). The new feminism. New York: Franklin Watts.</p>\n<p>Books (Two to three authors)</p>\n<p>Strunk, W., Jr., &amp; White, E.B. (1979).The elements of style (3rded.).</p>\n<p>New York: Macmillan.</p>\n<p>Journal Article (One author)</p>\n<p>Maki, R.H.(1982). Categorization effects which occur in comparative judgment tasks.</p>\n<p>Memory &amp; Cognition, 10, 252-264.</p>\n<p>Journal Article (Two authors)</p>\n<p>Atkinson, R.C., &amp;Shiffrin, R.M. (1971).The control of short-term memory.Scientific</p>\n<p>American, 225, 82-90</p>\n<p>Magazine Article (No author)</p>\n<p>The blood business (1992, September 11). Time, 97, 47-48.</p>\n<p>Magazine Article (One author)</p>\n<p>Kandel, E.R.(2000, November 10). Neuroscience: Breaking down scientific barriers</p>\n<p>to the study of brain and mind. Science, 290, 1113 - 1120.</p>\n<p>Encyclopedia Article</p>\n<p>Warren,S.A. (1977). Mental retardation and environment. In International encyclopedia of</p>\n<p>psychiatry, psychology, psychoanalysis, and neurology (vol.7,pp.202 -207). New York:</p>\n<p>Aesculapius Publishers.</p>\n<p>Newspaper Article</p>\n<p>Amazing Amazon region.(1989, January 12). New York Times, pp.D11,D14</p>\n<p>Electronic Journal Article</p>\n<p>Vanden,G., Knap, S., &amp; Doe, J. (2001). Role of reference elements in the selection</p>\n<p>of resources by psychology undergraduates.{Electronic Reference version}.</p>\n<p>Electronic Referencing</p>\n<p>Thaller, M. (2007). Cool cosmos. Retrieved August 27, 2007 from</p>\n<p>http:/coolcosmos.ipac.caltech.edu/.</p>\n<p>Note: For details and guidance, search for APA referencing style on the internet.</p>\n<p>b. Appendices</p>\n<p>There are various items that a researcher does not want to include in the body of</p>\n<p>the report, such things as raw data, transmittal letter, certain maps and charts,</p>\n<p>detailed calculations, instruments etcare put under an appendix. Appendices are</p>\n<p>put after the final chapter and references.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">REFERENCES</h2>\n<p>Awoniyi, S. A., Aderanti, R. A. &amp;Tayo, A. S. (2011).Introduction to Research Methods.</p>\n<p>Ibadan: Ababa Press Ltd</p>\n<p>Awoniyi, S. A. and Alege, B. A. (Ed) (2007).General Education Studies for Prospective</p>\n<p>Teacher.Ilorin, Nigeria: Samadex prints. Pp. 21-51</p>\n<p>Best, J. W. and Kahn, J.V. (1993).Research in Education. U.S.A: Allyn and Bacon.</p>\n<p>Cooper, D. R &amp; Schindler, P. A. (2008). Business Research Methods. Toronto: McGraw</p>\n<p>Hill Emory, C.M. (1995) Business Research Methods. Irwin: Homewood</p>\n<p>Flynn, J. Z. &amp; Foster, I. M. (2009).Research Methods for the Fashion Industry. New York:</p>\n<p>Fairchild Books</p>\n<p>Gay, L. R. (1981). Educational Research.Second Edition.Columbus: Charles E Merril.</p>\n<p>Kothari, C. R. (1998). Research Methodology: Methods and Techniques. Pune: Wishwa</p>\n<p>Prakashan</p>\n<p>Peil, M (1995). Social Science Research Methods: A handbook for Africa. Nairobi: East</p>\n<p>Africa Educational Publishers Ltd.</p>\n<p>Punch, K. F. (2005). Introduction to Social Research.New Delhi: SAGE publications.</p>\n<p>Rosenthal, R. &amp;Rosnow, R. L. (2008).Essentials of Behavioral Research: Methods and</p>\n<p>Analysis. Toronto: McGraw Hill</p>\n<p>Sevilla, G.C et al (1992). Research Methods. Quezon City : Rex Printing Company.</p>\n<p>Inc. Zulueta, F. M. and Costales, N. B. (2004).Methods of Research Thesis Writing and</p>\n<p>Applied</p>\n<p>Statistics.MandaluyongCity: National Book store.</p>\n<p>Any other Research reading materials</p>";
